{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_014wnxQS1GnYu7PCjW9n7e36","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# C→Rust translation conventions (PCRE2, 8-bit mode)\n2\t\n3\tYou are translating **one C file (or one range of one C file)** of PCRE2 into Rust.\n4\tThe Rust crate lives in `translation/` (`cargo build --release` from inside it).\n5\t**Never modify anything in `c_src/`.** Never edit another module's file unless your\n6\ttask says so.\n7\t\n8\tThe build configuration that must be assumed everywhere:\n9\t\n10\t* `PCRE2_CODE_UNIT_WIDTH == 8` → `PCRE2_UCHAR = u8`, `PCRE2_SPTR = *const u8`\n11\t* `LINK_SIZE == 2`, `IMM2_SIZE == 2`\n12\t* `SUPPORT_UNICODE` **defined** (so `SUPPORT_WIDE_CHARS` and `MAYBE_UTF_MULTI` are defined)\n13\t* `SUPPORT_JIT` **not** defined, `EBCDIC` **not** defined, `PCRE2_DEBUG` **not** defined,\n14\t  `HAVE_BUILTIN_MUL_OVERFLOW`/`HAVE_BUILTIN_UNREACHABLE`/`HAVE_ATTRIBUTE_UNINITIALIZED` **not** defined\n15\t* `PCRE2_ASSERT(x)`, `PCRE2_UNREACHABLE()`, `PCRE2_DEBUG_UNREACHABLE()` are **no-ops** – drop them\n16\t  (or write `/* PCRE2_ASSERT */`). Never insert a `panic!`/`unreachable!()` for them.\n17\t* Any code inside `#ifdef PCRE2_DEBUG`, `#ifdef SUPPORT_JIT`, `#ifdef EBCDIC`,\n18\t  `#if PCRE2_CODE_UNIT_WIDTH != 8`, `#ifdef SUPPORT_VALGRIND`, `#ifdef PCRE2_PCRE2TEST`\n19\t  is **not** compiled: skip it. Code in `#ifdef SUPPORT_UNICODE`, `#ifdef SUPPORT_WIDE_CHARS`,\n20\t  `#if PCRE2_CODE_UNIT_WIDTH == 8` **is** compiled.\n21\t\n22\t## Fidelity rules (most important)\n23\t\n24\t1. Translate **literally**, statement by statement, in the same order. Preserve the exact\n25\t   order of error checks, the exact error codes and the exact arithmetic.\n26\t2. **Do not fix bugs**, do not \"improve\", do not reorder, do not add bounds checks,\n27\t   do not add early returns.\n28\t3. Integer arithmetic must behave like C: use the same widths (`i32` for `int`,\n29\t   `u32` for `uint32_t`, `usize` for `size_t`/`PCRE2_SIZE`) and insert explicit\n30\t   `as` casts exactly where C's implicit conversions happen. The release profile has\n31\t   `overflow-checks = false`, so `+`/`-`/`*` wrap like C. Where C relies on wrapping of\n32\t   *unsigned* values, plain operators are fine; use `wrapping_*` if a value can overflow\n33\t   in a way that would trip a debug assertion.\n34\t4. No `panic!`, `unwrap()` on `Option<T>` other than function pointers, `assert!`,\n35\t   `expect`, or slice indexing that can go out of bounds. Use raw pointers.\n36\t5. Keep the C control flow. `goto` is emulated (see below).\n37\t\n38\t## Naming and layout\n39\t\n40\t* Your module file is `translation/src/<module>.rs`; it already contains stub definitions\n41\t  for every **exported** symbol with the exact `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn\n42\t  <linker_name>(...)` signature. **Replace the stub body; never change the name or the\n43\t  signature.**\n44\t* C file-`static` functions become `pub(crate) unsafe fn <same_name>(...)` (same name as in C).\n45\t  Make them `pub(crate)` (not private) so that other chunks of the same C file can call them.\n46\t* C file-`static` const tables become `static <NAME>: [T; N] = [...];` (no `#[no_mangle]`).\n47\t* C file-`static` mutable state: there is none that matters; if you find one, use\n48\t  `static mut` + `unsafe`.\n49\t* Add `use crate::consts::*; use crate::types::*; use crate::macros::*;` (already in the stub)\n50\t  plus whatever `use crate::<other_module>::<fn>;` you need. All cross-module (i.e. `PRIV(...)`)\n51\t  functions live in other modules under their **linker name**, e.g.\n52\t  `crate::string_utils::_pcre2_strcmp_c8_8`, `crate::newline::_pcre2_is_newline_8`,\n53\t  `crate::tables::_pcre2_OP_lengths_8`, `crate::ucd::_pcre2_ucd_records_8`.\n54\t\n55\t## Type mapping\n56\t\n57\t| C | Rust |\n58\t|---|---|\n59\t| `BOOL` (`int`), `TRUE`, `FALSE` | `BOOL` (= `i32`), `TRUE`, `FALSE` (from `consts`) |\n60\t| `int`, `int32_t` | `i32` |\n61\t| `unsigned int`, `uint32_t` | `u32` |\n62\t| `uint8_t`, `PCRE2_UCHAR` | `u8` |\n63\t| `uint16_t` | `u16` |\n64\t| `size_t`, `PCRE2_SIZE` | `usize` |\n65\t| `int64_t` / `INT64_OR_DOUBLE` | `i64` |\n66\t| `PCRE2_SPTR` | `*const u8` |\n67\t| `PCRE2_UCHAR *` | `*mut u8` |\n68\t| `void *` | `*mut core::ffi::c_void` |\n69\t| `const char *` | `*const core::ffi::c_char` |\n70\t| struct types (`compile_block`, `heapframe`, ...) | identically named `#[repr(C)]` structs in `crate::types` |\n71\t\n72\t`BOOL`-returning functions return `1`/`0`; write `if cond { 1 } else { 0 }` or\n73\t`(cond) as BOOL`. Test them with `!= 0`.\n74\t\n75\t## Pointers\n76\t\n77\t* `p++` → `p = p.add(1)`; `*p++` → `{ let t = *p; p = p.add(1); t }`.\n78\t* `p[i]` → `*p.add(i as usize)`; when `i` may be negative use `*p.offset(i as isize)`.\n79\t* `p - 1` where the result may be before the start of the object → `p.wrapping_sub(1)`\n80\t  (also `wrapping_add`/`wrapping_offset`). Prefer these in loops that decrement past\n81\t  the start, which C does routinely.\n82\t* Pointer difference `a - b` → `a.offset_from(b)` (an `isize`) or\n83\t  `(a as usize - b as usize)`; pick whatever keeps the C types (`PCRE2_SIZE`, `int`, ...).\n84\t* Casting a `*const u8` to `*mut u8`: `p as *mut u8`. Casting between struct pointers:\n85\t  `p as *mut heapframe`, `(p as *mut u8).add(n) as *mut heapframe`, etc.\n86\t* `memcpy(d,s,n)` → `core::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(s as *const u8, d as *mut u8, n)`\n87\t  (`memmove` → `core::ptr::copy`, `memset(d,c,n)` → `core::ptr::write_bytes(d as *mut u8, c as u8, n)`).\n88\t  `memcmp` → compare bytes in a loop or `crate::support::memcmp`-style helper you write locally.\n89\t* `strlen`/`strcmp` on `const char*`: write a tiny local loop.\n90\t\n91\t## Memory allocation\n92\t\n93\t`code->memctl.malloc(size, code->memctl.memory_data)` becomes\n94\t\n95\t```rust\n96\t((*code).memctl.malloc.unwrap())(size, (*code).memctl.memory_data)\n97\t```\n98\t\n99\tand `free` likewise. (`malloc`/`free` fields are `Option<unsafe extern \"C\" fn ...>` and are\n100\tnever null in practice.) The default allocators are\n101\t`crate::context::default_malloc` / `crate::context::default_free`.\n102\t\n103\t## Macros\n104\t\n105\t`crate::macros` provides the C macros; they are exported crate-wide (`#[macro_use]`),\n106\tso call them as `GET!(...)`, `PUT!(...)`, etc. Differences from C:\n107\t\n108\t* `GET!(a, n)`, `GET2!(a, n)` → `u32`; `PUT!(a, n, d)`, `PUT2!(a, n, d)`, `PUTINC!(a,n,d)`,\n109\t  `PUT2INC!(a,n,d)` (the latter two assign to `a`, so `a` must be a mutable place).\n110\t* `GETCHARINC!(c, eptr)`, `GETCHAR!(c, eptr)`, `GETCHARLEN!(c, eptr, len)` – same as C.\n111\t* The `...TEST` variants take `utf` explicitly:\n112\t  `GETCHARINCTEST!(c, eptr, utf)`, `GETCHARTEST!(c, eptr, utf)`, `GETCHARLENTEST!(c, eptr, len, utf)`.\n113\t* `PUTCHAR!(c, p, utf)` returns `u32`.\n114\t* `TABLE_GET!(c, table, default)`, `MAX_255!(c)` (always 1), `CHMAX_255!(c)`,\n115\t  `HAS_EXTRALEN!(c)`, `GET_EXTRALEN!(c)`, `NOT_FIRSTCU!(c)`, `SETBIT!(a,b)`,\n116\t  `MAPBIT!(map,n)`, `MAPSET!(map,n)`, `BACKCHAR!(p)`, `FORWARDCHAR!(p)`,\n117\t  `FORWARDCHARTEST!(p,end)`, `ACROSSCHAR!(cond, p, stmt)`.\n118\t* UCD: `GET_UCD!(ch)` (→ `*const ucd_record`), `UCD_CHARTYPE!`, `UCD_SCRIPT!`,\n119\t  `UCD_CATEGORY!`, `UCD_GRAPHBREAK!`, `UCD_CASESET!`, `UCD_OTHERCASE!`, `UCD_SCRIPTX!`,\n120\t  `UCD_BPROPS!`, `UCD_BIDICLASS!`, `UCD_SCRIPTX_PROP!`, `UCD_BIDICLASS_PROP!`,\n121\t  `UCD_BPROPS_PROP!`, `UCD_ANY_I!`, `UCD_DOTTED_I!`, `UCD_FOLD_I_TURKISH!`.\n122\t* Parsed-pattern offsets: `PUTOFFSET!(s,p)`, `GETOFFSET!(s,p)`, `GETPLUSOFFSET!(s,p)`,\n123\t  `READPLUSOFFSET!(s,p)`, `SKIPOFFSET!(p)`, `SIZEOFFSET` (= 2),\n124\t  `META_CODE!(x)`, `META_DATA!(x)`, `META_DIFF!(x,y)`.\n125\t* `IS_NEWLINE(p)` / `WAS_NEWLINE(p)`: use the helpers\n126\t  `is_newline_block(p, nltype, &mut nllen, nl_ptr, psend, utf)` and\n127\t  `was_newline_block(p, nltype, &mut nllen, nl_ptr, psstart, utf)` from `crate::macros`,\n128\t  passing the fields of your module's NLBLOCK (e.g. `(*mb).nltype`, `&mut (*mb).nllen`,\n129\t  `(*mb).nl.as_ptr()`, `(*mb).end_subject`). It is fine to define a local\n130\t  `macro_rules! IS_NEWLINE` wrapper at the top of your module.\n131\t* `CHAR_xxx` constants: there is no table of them; write the literal ASCII value the C\n132\t  compiler would use, e.g. `CHAR_A` → `b'A'` / `0x41`, `CHAR_LEFT_CURLY_BRACKET` → `b'{'`.\n133\t  Keep the C name in a comment if it helps. Character literals in comparisons: cast so the\n134\t  types line up, e.g. `if c == b'a' as u32`.\n135\t* Local convenience `macro_rules!` are welcome for repetitive C macros defined inside the\n136\t  file you are translating (e.g. `ADD_ACTIVE`, `RMATCH`, `CHECKMEMCPY`, ...). Define them\n137\t  at the top of your module.\n138\t\n139\t## Emulating `goto`\n140\t\n141\tRust has no `goto`. Use these patterns, in order of preference:\n142\t\n143\t1. **Forward jump to the end of a block** → labeled block + `break 'label`.\n144\t   ```rust\n145\t   'skip: {  ...  if cond { break 'skip; }  ... }\n146\t   ```\n147\t2. **Backward jump to the top of a loop** → `loop { ... continue 'lab; }`.\n148\t3. **A label jumped to from several places, in the middle of a function** → put the\n149\t   label's code in a `pub(crate) unsafe fn` if the state it uses is small, **or** use a\n150\t   state machine:\n151\t   ```rust\n152\t   const L_TOP: u32 = 0; const L_FOO: u32 = 1; ...\n153\t   let mut state = L_TOP;\n154\t   'sm: loop { match state {\n155\t       L_TOP => { ... state = L_FOO; continue 'sm; }\n156\t       L_FOO => { ... }\n157\t       _ => {}\n158\t   } break; }\n159\t   ```\n160\t   Keep all the variables the states share declared before the loop.\n161\t4. `goto` out of a nested loop → labeled `break 'outer`.\n162\t\n163\tWhatever you choose, the executed sequence of statements must match C exactly.\n164\tAdd a comment `/* goto LABEL */` at each jump site.\n165\t\n166\t## Switch statements\n167\t\n168\tC `switch` falls through; Rust `match` does not. Translate fall-through explicitly\n169\t(duplicate the code, or use a labeled block, or `|` for empty-body case lists).\n170\t`switch` on a code unit: `match c as u32 { OP_CHAR => {...}, _ => {} }` — remember that the\n171\t`OP_*`, `META_*`, `ESC_*`, `PT_*`, `ucp_*` constants in `crate::consts` are `u32`, so cast\n172\tthe scrutinee (`*code as u32`) rather than the constants.\n173\t\n174\t## Verifying your work\n175\t\n176\t```\n177\tcd translation && cargo build --release 2>&1 | grep -E \"^(error|warning: unused)\" | head -40\n178\tcargo build --release 2>&1 | grep -A8 \"src/<yourfile>.rs\" | head -80\n179\t```\n180\t\n181\tYour file must produce **no errors**. Errors coming from *other* files are not yours\n182\t(other modules are still stubs). Do not silence errors by deleting code, and do not\n183\tleave `todo!()`/`unimplemented!()` behind.\n184\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Wr1AorXuSP6rGo93ZXv5Pr","session_id":"6b19aa16-17c8-42f3-a300-59c20a915dbc","uuid":"62268aee-c627-48a6-909a-f30b847d9e6c","timestamp":"2026-08-31T06:10:11.410Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Translate substitute"}
