{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ad1fa5a7f21559707","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01HTrGJwWjp3nTD3iwEFSgAn","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-xpOsEx/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-ZHr7ad/6b19aa16-17c8-42f3-a300-59c20a915dbc/tasks/ad1fa5a7f21559707.output","summary":"Both files translated and verified against the reference C library.\n\n**What I translated**\n\n`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-ZHr7ad/translation/src/config.rs` (from `c_src/src/pcre2_config.c`) — `pcre2_config_8` with both switches in the original order: the `where == NULL` length-request switch returning `size_of::<u32>()`, then the value switch. Build config baked in as specified: JIT not supported (`PCRE2_CONFIG_JIT` → 0, `PCRE2_CONFIG_JITTARGET` → `PCRE2_ERROR_BADOPTION`), Unicode supported (→ 1, version from `crate::ucd::_pcre2_unicode_version_8.0`), BSR_UNICODE, `CONFIGURED_LINK_SIZE` = 2 (local const, since `consts` has only `LINK_SIZE`), effective linksize = `LINK_SIZE * sizeof(PCRE2_UCHAR)` = 2, limits from `crate::consts`. I kept the C runtime prerelease-hack literally: three precomputed stringification statics (`\"Z -DEV\\0\"`, `\"10.48 2025-10-21\\0\"`, `\"10.48-DEV 2025-10-21\\0\"`) and the `[1] == 0` test, so the selected version string is `10.48-DEV 2025-10-21`. String cases use `crate::string_utils::_pcre2_strcpy_c8_8` plus a local `strlen` for the NULL case.\n\n`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-ZHr7ad/translation/src/error.rs` (from `c_src/src/pcre2_error.c`) — both message tables as single NUL-separated byte-string literals (`static compile_error_texts: &[u8] = b\"...\";`, same for `match_error_texts`), generated mechanically from the C source rather than hand-typed, with the `XSTRING` inserts resolved (`UTF-8 mode`, `maximum 128 code units`, `maximum 10000`) and the non-EBCDIC variant of message 168. Crucially I had to append an explicit extra `\\0`: in C these are string literals, so the compiler adds the implicit terminator that makes the table end in `\\0\\0`, which is exactly what the counting loop detects. `pcre2_get_error_message_8` is a statement-for-statement translation, including the empty-message-list branch for invalid (0..99) error numbers.\n\n**Verification**\n\nI compiled the same C harness twice — once against `_cbuild/libpcre2.so` and once against the Rust `cdylib` — and diffed. 364 lines of output identical, covering: `pcre2_config` for `what` = 0..17 both with `NULL` (length request) and with a buffer; `pcre2_get_error_message` for every error number from -80 to 250; buffer sizes 0..12 to exercise the truncation/`PCRE2_ERROR_NOMEMORY` path; and invalid `what` values (2, 17, 18, 100, `0xFFFFFFFF`) with a non-NULL buffer. No mismatches were found, so nothing needed fixing after the fact — generating the tables from the C source instead of retyping them is what avoided that. Two config values worth flagging because they are build-dependent and I matched the actual `_cbuild` configuration: `PCRE2_CONFIG_COMPILED_WIDTHS` is **0** (this build does not define `SUPPORT_PCRE2_8/16/32`, only `PCRE2_CODE_UNIT_WIDTH=8`), and `PCRE2_CONFIG_TABLES_LENGTH` is 1088. Scratch files and the throwaway C programs were deleted; nothing in `c_src/` was modified.\n\nFinal build with the requested command: clean, zero errors and zero warnings from `src/config.rs` or `src/error.rs`.\n\nresult: pcre2_config.c and pcre2_error.c translated to translation/src/config.rs and src/error.rs; build clean and output byte-identical to libpcre2.so across all config queries and error numbers -80..250.","usage":{"total_tokens":67583,"tool_uses":30,"duration_ms":292302},"uuid":"9d29a3aa-7155-40b9-ba9e-8fca00d34696","session_id":"6b19aa16-17c8-42f3-a300-59c20a915dbc"}
