nthetic state constants you need, numbered\n   1000..1099 (yours), e.g. `pub const L_A_CHARLOOP: u32 = 1000;`\n\nTRANSLATION RULES for the state machine (these are the crux of the task):\n * `RMATCH(ra, RMn)` becomes:\n       start_ecode = ra; Freturn_id!() = RMn as u8; state = S_MATCH_RECURSE; continue 'sm;\n   and everything the C code executes AFTER that RMATCH (up to the end of the enclosing\n   construct) goes into the state `RMn => { ... }`.\n * `RRETURN(x)` becomes:  rrc = x; state = S_RETURN_SWITCH; continue 'sm;\n * A C `break` that leaves the switch (i.e. \"go round the main loop again\") and a C\n   `continue` of the main `for(;;)` both become:  state = S_MAINLOOP; continue 'sm;\n   (An arm that simply ends also falls through to the main loop, which is correct - but\n   prefer being explicit.)\n * `goto LABEL` becomes:  state = L_LABEL; continue 'sm;\n * If an RMATCH sits inside a C loop, do NOT duplicate the loop body: create a synthetic\n   state for the loop entry (EXTRA_STATE_CONSTS) so that the resume state can jump back\n   to it, e.g.\n       C:  for (;;) { A; RMATCH(x, RM2); B; }\n       Rust:  L_A_LOOP => { A; start_ecode = x; Freturn_id!() = RM2 as u8;\n                            state = S_MATCH_RECURSE; continue 'sm; }\n              RM2      => { B; state = L_A_LOOP; continue 'sm; }\n * The frame fields have the same short names as in C, as macros used as places:\n   Fecode!(), Feptr!(), Fop!(), Fovector!() (a *mut PCRE2_SIZE: use `*Fovector!().add(n)`),\n   Foffset_top!(), Fcapture_last!(), Fcurrent_recurse!(), Fgroup_frame_type!(),\n   Flast_group_offset!(), Fmark!(), Frdepth!(), Fstart_match!(), Fback_frame!(),\n   Freturn_id!(), Fback_frame!().\n   The `Lxxx` #defines that the C code makes inside individual cases refer to the fields\n   union: write them out, e.g. `Lstart_eptr` -> `(*F).fields.char_repeat.start_eptr`,\n   `Lmin` -> `(*F).fields.char_repeat.min`, `Lc` -> `(*F).fields.char_repeat.c`,\n   `Loc` -> `(*F).fields.char_repeat.oc.oc`, `Locchars` -> `(*F).fields.char_repeat.oc.occu`,\n   `Lbyte_map_address` -> `(*F).fields.class_repeat.byte_map_address`, etc. Check\n   crate::types (heapframe_fields / hf_* structs) for the exact field names - they match\n   the C union member names exactly.\n * CHECK_PARTIAL!() and SCHECK_PARTIAL!() macros exist (they may `return`).\n * IS_NEWLINE!(p) / WAS_NEWLINE!(p) macros exist.\n * Available locals: F, N, P, frames_top, assert_accept_frame, frame_copy_size,\n   branch_end, branch_start, bracode, offset, length, rrc, proptype, i, fc, number,\n   reptype, group_frame_type, condition, cur_is_word, prev_is_word, utf, ucp,\n   start_ecode, frame_size, top_bracket, match_data, mb.\n * Helper functions in crate::matcher: do_callout, match_ref, recurse_update_offsets\n   (being written by another agent in parallel - call them as\n   `crate::matcher::do_callout(...)` etc.; unresolved-name errors for them are expected\n   and should be reported).\n * Other useful items: crate::tables::_pcre2_OP_lengths_8, crate::xclass::{_pcre2_xclass_8,\n   _pcre2_eclass_8}, crate::extuni::_pcre2_extuni_8, crate::script_run::_pcre2_script_run_8,\n   crate::ucd::*, UCD macros, crate::consts::* , rep_min/rep_max/rep_typ, GF_* , MATCH_* ,\n   RECURSE_UNSET (all defined in the skeleton, which is the same module).\n\nBUILD/VERIFY LOOP:\n  cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-ZHr7ad\n  python3 _gen/assemble_match.py            # splices fragments into translation/src/match_engine.rs\n  cd translation && cargo build --release 2>&1 | grep -E \"error\" -A6 | head -60\nNever edit translation/src/match_engine.rs directly (it is generated) and never edit\n_gen/frag_match_B.rs or _gen/frag_match_C.rs (other agents own them). Errors coming from\nthe other chunks' arms/states are not yours.\n\nWork through your range in sequential pieces, re-reading the C source for each piece.\nDo not modify anything in c_src/.\n\nReport: the C line range you covered, the list of arms and states